A||||2||turbo86se||Selling a leased vehicle||||5||
Z||000000||turbo86se||06-27-2000||09:37 PM||||These past few weeks I've been thinking about selling my leased Altima. If I were to sell it, basically have someone take over payments, it would tremendously help me out for the next two years, since I'll be in school full-time, getting my AA in Criminal Justice. Can I just simply have someone take over payments? How would this affect my credit, if at all?<BR>It's a three-year lease, 2000 Nissan Altima GLE, pearl white, tan leather, all options. I'm on my seventh month, and I'm 2000 miles under for the lease agreement.<BR>Thanks for your help!  BTW, anyone interested?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>--joel||||reg||5
Z||000001||zeusrekning||06-27-2000||10:34 PM||||how's it going joel? ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||06-28-2000||02:24 AM||||It will all depend on your leasing company. Some do and some don't. But the way it works is you find someone to take over payments. They have to fill out a credit application and go through everything you went through.<BR>If their credit is good, They can get the car. The big catch is if they default on payments the leasing company looks for you. <BR>The contract still is in your name. If you've only had the car 7 months you may want to call the leasing company and ask them what your present Buy-Out is. If the Buy-Out is close to what the vehicle is selling for you can sell it for that price. That way the person buying it will own it and you will be clear of the lease.||||reg||1
